Research spikes #2 + #3, follow-up to sm_inverse_decimation_spike.

SPIKE #2 (base-sweep): rather than test one fractal at a time, sweep the
small-λ decimation slope b ∈ [1.5, 500] continuously and look for any b
that makes SM mass² ratios sit on integer log_b levels.

  Result: per-family fits look stunning
    leptons     b* = 1.8725, k=(17, 9),  dev = 0.0007
    up_quarks   b* = 2.667,  k=(13, 10), dev = 0.0079
    down_quarks b* = 1.7212, k=(11, 14), dev = 0.0168

  But b's differ across families; universal b* = 1.5321 gives only borderline
  dev 0.1002 (just at survival threshold).

SPIKE #3 (NULL TEST): 10,000 synthetic "fermion families" with random
mass² ratios drawn from same log-magnitude distribution as SM, run same
base-sweep, compare distribution to SM observed devs.

  Null distribution:
    1st pct: 0.00032   median: 0.00827   90th pct: 0.01737

  SM observed vs null percentile:
    leptons     dev 0.0007 → 3% (suggestive but isolated)
    up_quarks   dev 0.0079 → 48% (noise)
    down_quarks dev 0.0168 → 89% (worse than random)
    JOINT       1.22% under null (suggestive but weak)

VERDICT: the base-sweep diagnostic CANNOT distinguish real fractal
structure from Diophantine approximation luck. The spike #2 "per-family
clean fits" are mostly chance — up- and down-quark devs are squarely
within the null distribution.

On the §XIII.1 framework: this does NOT disprove §XIII.1; it shows
that the search method (eigenvalue-ratio matching) is too information-
poor to identify THE fractal. A real §XIII.1 test would need to match
SIMULTANEOUSLY:
  1. Eigenvalue ratios (what we tested)
  2. Eigenvalue multiplicities → SM gauge-irrep dimensions
  3. Spectral dimension d_S(σ) profile (UV→2, IR→4 per framework)
  4. Exceptional-eigenvalue structure
  5. 3-fold structural self-similarity (3 generations as feature)

We tested 1 of 5; that's underdetermined and degenerate.

Next research move (if pressing): SPIKE #4 — multiplicity test.
Search for fractal × G/H combinations where eigenvalue multiplicities
match SM gauge irrep dimensions {1, 2, 3, 8} (U(1), SU(2), fundamental
SU(3), adjoint SU(3)). Discrete-structure check; chance can't fake it
as easily as a continuous-b sweep.

On user's "fooled by Gemini-CLI" question: partial answer — the
eigenvalue-matching method as applied was naïve. The framework's
predictions are MORE specific than what we tested. Continued pressing
should target the more discriminating constraints (multiplicity,
spectral dimension), not the easy one (ratios) we exhausted here.

